%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%
%%% Section: Introduction
%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
This report is an example of reproducible research using the function \Rfun{knit}, in the \R\ package \Rpackage{knitr}, and \LaTeX. For an introduction to \Rpackage{knitr}, I recommend the website of the package\cite{knitr}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%
%%% Subsection: 
%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Toy example}
\label{sec:toy}
The aim of this document is to provide a minimal example of a \Robject{Rnw} source file that integrates code of both \R\ and \LaTeX, which result is this PDF file. The statistical analysis shown in this document, a linear regression fitting, is only for illustration purposes on how \Robject{knitr} works  and should not be considered for statistical purposes. In Section \ref{sec:data} we explore data graphically. Then, in Section \ref{sec:modeling}, we fit and interpret a linear regression model. The \R\ code used is shown in Appendix \ref{app:Rcode}. Appendix \ref{app:libraries} shows the \R\ packages we need to get the PDF for this document.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%
%%% Section: Data
%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Data}
\label{sec:data}
Figure \ref{fig:dataplot}\footnote{See Appendix \ref{app:data}.} shows a graphical representation of the relationship between the wind speed and ozone levels, where we can see a possible linear association:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:model}
\text{Ozone} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Wind speed}
\end{equation}


<<dataplot, fig.pos='ht', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=8, out.width='0.45\\textwidth', fig.cap='\\label{fig:dataplot}Scatter plot of ozone levels vs. wind speed.'>>=

### scatter plot ozone vs. wind:
par(las = 1, cex.lab = 1.3, cex.axis = 1.3)
plot(airquality$Wind,
     airquality$Ozone,
     xlab = "Wind speed (mph)",
     ylab = "Ozone levels (ppb)",
     pch = 19,
     col = "gray")
@



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%
%%% Section: Modeling
%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Modeling}
\label{sec:modeling}


<<modeling>>=
### linear regression model fitting:
mod <- lm(Ozone ~ Wind, data = airquality)
### get coefficients table:
coefs <- summary(mod)$coefficients
### get beta of interest:
beta <- coefs["Wind", "Estimate"]
@


<<betaformat>>=
### output formatting: store some stuff related to the beta of interest, to be
### used with \Sexpr{} in the report:
# first, we store the absolute value of beta:
betaabs <- abs(beta)
# now, we format it to be printed with 2 decimal places using sprintf:
betafancy <- sprintf("%.2f", betaabs)
# now we want to use "increase" or "decrease" in the report depending on if
# beta is positive or negative, respectively. We store it in the object "eff"
# (for "effect"):
eff <- ifelse(beta > 0, "increase", "decrease")
@

In Section \ref{sec:data} we have seen that a there is a possible linear relationship between the wind speed and the ozone levels, under model \eqref{eq:model}. Thus, we can try to fit a linear regression model\footnote{See \R\ code in Appendix \ref{app:model}.}. Specifically, Table \ref{tab:coeftable} shows the estimated coefficients of the linear model\footnote{The vignette ``The \Rpackage{xtable}\ Gallery''\cite{xtablegallery} provides a lot of examples for table customization.}. Under this model, a one unit increase in the wind speed is associated to a \Sexpr{eff} of \Sexpr{betafancy} units in the mean of ozone levels. Figure \ref{fig:modelplot} shows the data as well as the fitted model.

<<mytable, results = 'asis'>>=
# exporting coefficients table to LaTeX format:
modtable <- xtable(coefs,
                   digits = c(0, 2, 2, 2, 4),
                   caption = "Linear regression model coefficients for wind and ozone association.",
                   label = "tab:coeftable")
# cosmetics for table head (LaTeX slash (\) must be doubled inside a R chunk):
colnames(modtable) <- c("\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Estimate}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Std.~Error}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\(\\boldsymbol{t}\\)\\bf{-value}}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\(\\boldsymbol{p}\\)\\bf{-value}}")

# format and print the table:
print(modtable,
      table.placement = "ht",
      size = "footnotesize",
      include.rownames = TRUE,
      include.colnames = TRUE,
      floating = TRUE,                 # whether \begin{table} should be created (TRUE) or not (FALSE)
      sanitize.text.function = force,  # important to treat content of columns as latex function
      booktabs = TRUE)                 # requires \usepackage{booktabs} in the preamble of the document
@


<<modelplot, fig.pos='ht', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=8, out.width='0.45\\textwidth', fig.cap=paste0("\\label{fig:modelplot}Scatter plot of ozone levels vs. wind speed and the fitted linear regression model for the mean of ozone levels as a function of wind speed. In the fitted model, \\(\\hat{\\beta}_0 = ", sprintf("%.2f", coef(mod)[1]), "\\) ppb and \\(\\hat{\\beta}_1 = ", sprintf("%.2f", coef(mod)[2]), "\\) ppb/mph.")>>=
# scatter plot ozone vs. wind again (recycling code in chunk dataplot):

# we increase the font sizes to compensate the reduction that dev='tikz'
# introduces: 
par(cex = 1.4)

# reuse the previous chunk named "dataplot":
<<dataplot>>

# add the fitted model line:
abline(mod, col = "blue", lwd = 2)

# add legend:
legend("topright",
       legend = c("Data", "Fitted model"),
       col = c("gray", "blue"),
       lwd = c(0, 2),   # line width 
       pch = c(19, NA), # point symbol
       bty = "n",       # no box around the legend
       cex = 1.1)       # font size (multiplier)
@

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%
%%% Section: Extracting
%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Extracting \R\ code}
\label{sec:r}
You can extract the \R\ code included in the \Robject{Rnw} source document to a \R\ file by using the \Rfun{purl} function in the \Rpackage{knitr} package:

<<r, echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
library(knitr)
purl("knitr_example.Rnw")
@

Read \Rcode{?purl} to see how the \R\ code extraction can be customized (e.g., which chunks must be extracted, split \R\ files, file name or file documentation)


